{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "the social network is a thought-provoking film that delves into the fascinating story of facebook's creation and the tumultuous journey of its founder, mark zuckerberg. directed by david fincher, the movie is a gripping narrative that explores the complexities of ambition, friendship, and the consequences of success.\n\nthe film begins with mark zuckerberg, played by jesse eisenberg, as a sophomore at harvard university, where he creates a website called \"facemash\" that allows users to compare the photos of two students and vote on which one is more attractive. this initial success sparks a chain of events that ultimately leads to the creation of facebook.\n\nas the story unfolds, we see mark's relationships with his friends and fellow students, including eduardo savernin, played by andrew garfield, and the winklevoss twins, played by armie hammer. the dynamics between these characters are complex and multifaceted, with each one bringing their own unique perspective to the story.\n\none of the standout aspects of the film is its exploration of the themes that drive mark's actions. is he motivated by a desire to revolutionize the way people connect with each other, or is he simply driven by a need for validation and success? the movie raises important questions about the nature of ambition and the consequences of allowing it to consume us.\n\nthe cast delivers impressive performances throughout the film, with eisenberg bringing a nuanced and complex portrayal to the role of mark zuckerberg. garfield is equally impressive as eduardo, bringing a sense of vulnerability and intensity to the character. the chemistry between the two actors is palpable, and their performances are some of the most compelling aspects of the film.\n\nthe direction by fincher is equally impressive, with a keen eye for detail and a deep understanding of the story he's trying to tell. the film's cinematography is sleek and modern, capturing the fast-paced and ever-changing world of technology and social media.\n\nthe score by trent reznor and atticus ross is also noteworthy, providing a haunting and atmospheric soundtrack that perfectly complements the tone of the film. the music is often minimalist and introspective, adding to the overall sense of unease and tension that pervades the story.\n\none of the most striking aspects of the film is its portrayal of the consequences of mark's actions. as facebook grows in popularity, mark becomes increasingly isolated and withdrawn, struggling to cope with the pressures of his newfound success. the film raises important questions about the cost of ambition and the impact it can have on our personal relationships and our sense of identity.\n\noverall, the social network is a thought-provoking and engaging film that offers a fascinating glimpse into the world of facebook and its founder. with its complex characters, nuanced themes, and impressive performances, it's a movie that will stay with you long after the credits roll.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.9773347973823547}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0004104725376237184}, {"token": " network", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.01104797888547182}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " thought", "logprob": -1.6509639024734497}, {"token": "-prov", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oking", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.022366993129253387}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.0015046000480651855}, {"token": " delves", "logprob": -0.31805509328842163}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -1.0132738680113107e-05}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " fascinating", "logprob": -0.5878432989120483}, {"token": " story", "logprob": -0.0033906372264027596}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.004078404977917671}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0006444760947488248}, {"token